2 of our Cloud SQL product.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.150
Do we want talk a little
bit about what Cloud SQL is?

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:35.066
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sure.

00:02:35.066 --> 00:02:38.940
So Cloud SQL is basically
just MySQL on the cloud.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:39.690
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:40.280
That's really it.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:41.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
pretty much what it is.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.470
It has some really
cool features,

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.850
like the fact that
you don't need to care

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:47.780
about that instance working.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:48.490
That's on us.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:51.150
So you basically say, hey,
I need one instance running.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.200
And if there's any patches
or security or whatever

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.210
it needs to be done, it's Google
engineers will do that for you.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:57.550
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.780
And if you want to replicate
it and do that stuff like that,

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.074
it's sort of just push button.

00:03:01.074 --> 00:03:01.990
It's just really easy.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:02.906
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:02.906 --> 00:03:06.950
Everything is-- basically,
it's like a self-driving MySQL

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.550
instance on the cloud.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:09.320
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:10.195
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:03:10.195 --> 00:03:14.730
And now we have version 2,
which is in beta and better.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:15.790
You see, with my accent?

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:16.498
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:03:16.498 --> 00:03:18.670
With your accent that
works, yeah, totally.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:22.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So what's
new with the beta version?

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.420
MARK MANDEL: So basically,
really, it is just better.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.320
So it's a lot faster.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.640
I have a number here.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.220
It was, I believe,
up to seven times

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.892
faster than the current
version, which is pretty good.

00:03:33.892 --> 00:03:35.350
And you know, like
you don't really

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.570
have to do very much
above and beyond that.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.450
And also, it takes-- it
scales out a whole lot better,

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.050
up to 20 times the storage
capacity of first generation.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:43.966
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:43.966 --> 00:03:45.520
I actually saw,
there's a graphic--

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.800
we'll put that in
the show notes here--

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.200
but there's a graphic where
it shows how much it scales.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.960
And when you have enough traffic
where having 64 course helps,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.460
you're going to see
that it scales--

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.260
it's much, much faster.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.920
It's able to have a way higher
transaction per second count

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.280
than v. 1.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.545
There's also, yeah, storage.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:08.764
It used to be 500
gigabytes, which is decent.

00:04:08.764 --> 00:04:09.930
MARK MANDEL: It's all right.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.326
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Now
it's 10 terabytes.

00:04:11.326 --> 00:04:12.450
MARK MANDEL: That's
pretty decent.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.116
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which
is pretty decent.

00:04:14.116 --> 00:04:17.070
That's a decent amount of data.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.690
So yeah, and that's
pretty much it.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.020
It's still beta, so all the
features are still not there.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:23.770
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:25.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
we're working on them.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:25.768
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:04:25.768 --> 00:04:28.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So if
you're interested in MySQL

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.260
and on the cloud,
just give it a try.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:30.525
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:30.525 --> 00:04:31.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Let
us know how it goes.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:32.110
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:32.820
It's pretty straightforward.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:33.820
When you go to
create your instance,

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:35.855
just choose second
generation and away you go.

00:04:35.855 --> 00:04:37.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Awesome, great.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.085
So let's go with our interview.

00:04:40.085 --> 00:04:41.925
MARK MANDEL: Let's
talk to Felipe.

00:04:41.925 --> 00:04:44.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we're here
with our colleague Felipe.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:44.981
Hi, Felipe.

00:04:44.981 --> 00:04:46.230
FELIPE HOFFA: Hello, Francesc.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.080
Hello, Mark.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.457
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
there is Felipe Hoffa.

00:04:49.457 --> 00:04:51.290
Tell us a little bit
about yourself, Felipe.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.670
Who are you, what do you do?

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:57.010
FELIPE HOFFA: My
name is Felipe Hoffa.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:05:00.290
I'm a cloud developer advocate.

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:05.470
I talk a lot about big
data, especially BigQuery.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:08.060
I started at Google
almost five years ago.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.850
Five years ago, I was
interviewing at this stage.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.230
I started as a
software engineer.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:14.759
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yes, like me right.

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:15.800
MARK MANDEL: Not like me.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:20.690
FELIPE HOFFA: Not like you,
yes, we have a similar story.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:23.990
And then, two years later, I
joined the developer relations

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.900
team, and I became a software
engineer with a license

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:29.520
to speak.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: With
a license to speak.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:31.660
MARK MANDEL: I like that.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:32.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's very modest.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:34.180
MARK MANDEL: I'm
going to use that.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.782
FELIPE HOFFA: I said it first.

00:05:35.782 --> 00:05:36.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.810
So you said that you'd
talk a lot about big data.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.330
So we're going to ask
you just to do that.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.735
Tell us what is big
data, for you at least?

00:05:44.735 --> 00:05:46.314
FELIPE HOFFA: What is big data?

00:05:46.314 --> 00:05:47.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.021
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:05:48.021 --> 00:05:50.479
A lot of people have tried to
who define what is big data.

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:52.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so
what's your definition?

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:54.000
MARK MANDEL: What
is the definition?

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:54.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Actually, yeah, what

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:56.660
is the absolute definition?

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.560
FELIPE HOFFA: OK,
we are at Google.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:05:59.320
We are at Google.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:02.040
You know how we call
big data at Google?

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.090
MARK MANDEL: Data.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:05.390
Nice.

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:07.790
FELIPE HOFFA: We call it data.

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:10.530
It being big is a
very weird concept.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:15.170
Data has no size or weight.

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:23.650
Some people called big data
problems that are complicated.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.780
For example, I
love to ask people

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:29.470
when I go to a conference,
who here works in big data?

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.876
Normally, people have
no idea if they do.

00:06:32.876 --> 00:06:33.542
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:06:35.859 --> 00:06:37.900
FELIPE HOFFA: But then
asked them questions like,

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.800
who knows SQL?

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.990
People that know
SQL are people that

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:44.460
are working with
data, with databases.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:45.630
MARK MANDEL: Fair enough.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:47.088
FELIPE HOFFA: Then
my next question

00:06:47.088 --> 00:06:51.540
is, who has had queries that
ran for more than an hour,

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:54.277
for a day, or that
simply don't run?

00:06:54.277 --> 00:06:55.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:56.420
I can see, yeah.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:57.100
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:02.150
FELIPE HOFFA: And that's when we
enter a different domain where

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:05.650
problems are taking a
long time, when problems

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:09.410
are complex to solve, at least
with the technologies they

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:10.047
know.

00:07:10.047 --> 00:07:10.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:11.546
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:07:11.546 --> 00:07:13.290
So rather than look
at it from like this

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:17.440
is how much terabytes or
petabytes or millions of rows,

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.190
rather look at it from
the perspective of,

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:21.150
I've got a problem,
I need to solve it,

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:22.420
when it has to do with data.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:25.140
But it's going to take
me three hours a day,

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.230
a week, given the tools
that I have at my disposal.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:29.180
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:31.800
That's what people think, oh,
this is that big data problem.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:32.700
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.000
FELIPE HOFFA: Then we
have the Google way

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.920
of solving problems we where
we bring the same problem here

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:44.162
with technologies we have
created, developed here.

00:07:44.162 --> 00:07:47.100
And the same problem
that might take you days

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:52.500
using your standard toolkit,
might take you 30 seconds.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Right, nice.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:57.730
So big data, finally,
it's not really

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:58.980
about the size of the data.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.242
It's really about
how much time it

00:08:01.242 --> 00:08:02.450
takes you to solve a problem.

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:05.790
And how like standard
techniques, at some point

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.910
they're just not-- they just
don't work anymore, right?

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.260
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:13.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So what kind
of techniques do you use then?

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:14.608
FELIPE HOFFA: More
than techniques.

00:08:14.608 --> 00:08:16.880
At this time it's
about what tools.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:23.870
FELIPE HOFFA: For a while, to
be able to manage terabytes,

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:27.750
of billions of rows, people had
to learn are new technologies.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:32.700
We have, for example, MapReduce,
Hadoop, [INAUDIBLE], et cetera.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:36.570
But then there is a lot
of complexity there.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:43.110
You need to wrap your mind
around using parallel servers,

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:46.050
using developing
[INAUDIBLE] algorithms,

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:48.410
but sometimes you just
want to count things.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:50.820
You just want to know what
are your top customers,

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.720
or how things are changing.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:57.020
So instead of going through
all these technologies,

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:00.440
today you can use technologies
like a query, where you just

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:03.220
load your data, ask
your question with SQL,

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:05.660
and you get the
answer in seconds,

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:09.820
without having to learn
a whole new mindset.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:10.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:15.280
So you mentioned that
one of the things that we

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.980
know about Google is we
have internal problems,

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:22.430
we solve them, and then we put
them as available to anyone

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:23.510
else outside.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:24.937
Is big query one of those?

00:09:24.937 --> 00:09:26.770
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah,
well, historically we've

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:28.750
been publishing
all these papers.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:33.230
The [INAUDIBLE] paper, et
cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:35.467
We've been through that, yes.

00:09:35.467 --> 00:09:37.550
And one of those people
of those papers is Dremel.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:43.060
How do we process terabytes
of data using SQL at scale?

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:44.930
And that paper explains
the whole detail

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:48.047
of how do we parallelize
this task without making it--

00:09:48.047 --> 00:09:48.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:51.180
We'll put that
definition-- yeah.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:52.180
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah, so--

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:53.410
MARK MANDEL: That's
really interesting.

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:55.035
FELIPE HOFFA: So
yeah, historically, we

00:09:55.035 --> 00:09:56.460
release the papers.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:00.190
And the world rushes
to implement them,

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:01.940
with open source
implementations.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:03.260
But now we have Google Cloud.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:05.890
Now we make these
services-- the same services

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.710
we use internally--
available for everyone.

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.300
And that's what we're
doing with BigQuery.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.304
That's the service that
implements the Dremel paper.

00:10:15.304 --> 00:10:17.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
something-- I actually

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.400
heard a story about Dremel.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.950
I don't know if it's true or
if it's just like a legend.

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:25.300
But I heard about the fact
that when Dremel was created

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:28.720
at Google, to process
24 hours of logs,

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:32.050
was taking almost
24 hours of logs,

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:35.880
which means that it's taking--
processing one hour of logs

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:37.920
takes more than one
hour, then you're

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:38.080
MARK MANDEL: You're going to--

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:38.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: At
some point, you're

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:40.161
going to have list [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:40.161 --> 00:10:44.810
Like is that true like the good
Dremel solves that problem?

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.880
Like all of a sudden,
instead of hours,

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.046
you go down to
minutes or seconds?

00:10:49.046 --> 00:10:50.540
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes, yes.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.136
I have no idea if that is true.

00:10:53.136 --> 00:10:54.465
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I've heard it.

00:10:54.465 --> 00:10:58.050
FELIPE HOFFA: I know it's
true for many customers.

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:02.520
I know-- I know people who
are working with huge data

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:07.210
sets that were only able to
process a small percentage

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:08.415
of their logs.

00:11:08.415 --> 00:11:10.785
And through processes
that ran all night

00:11:10.785 --> 00:11:12.350
and failed during the night.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:15.030
And suddenly, they try
BigQuery and wow, yeah,

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:18.285
it takes five seconds
and no babysitting

00:11:18.285 --> 00:11:20.032
of processes or anything.

00:11:20.032 --> 00:11:21.490
MARK MANDEL: So
big query really is

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:23.490
like an example of what you
were talking about before.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:24.980
People struggling
up against walls

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:27.690
of how much time processing
was going to take.

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:30.830
And suddenly going from hours,
days, weeks down to seconds.

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:31.580
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:32.130
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:33.588
FELIPE HOFFA: And
without involving

00:11:33.588 --> 00:11:37.160
any special knowledge.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:39.280
MARK MANDEL: It's just
SQL and that's it.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:40.030
FELIPE HOFFA: Just SQL, yes.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:40.450
MARK MANDEL: You don't
have to worry about--

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:42.790
FELIPE HOFFA: We are
just looking about data.

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:44.380
You have data, you
have questions.

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:44.700
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:45.880
FELIPE HOFFA: That's it.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:46.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
how do you-- how do

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:48.570
you put that data in BigQuery?

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:54.730
FELIPE HOFFA: There
are two main ways.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:55.660
One is batches.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:58.210
If you have a CSV
or JSON files, you

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:00.830
can load them into
Google Cloud Storage,

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:01.980
if they are big enough.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:05.430
So it's good to go through
Google Cloud Storage, when

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:07.120
you have very big files.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:09.420
You can store them there,
and BigQuery can easily

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:11.250
ingest files from these places.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:12.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:16.910
FELIPE HOFFA: Or also, big
query can ingest streaming data.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:21.070
So if you have any stream
of real time data coming,

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:27.280
you can load up to 100,000
rows per second into one

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:28.190
big query table.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:29.606
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That sounds a lot

00:12:29.606 --> 00:12:33.190
like what we were discussing
last episode about messaging.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.880
We were talking
about Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:35.671
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:12:35.671 --> 00:12:38.392
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Is that the way
to stream data into BigQuery?

00:12:38.392 --> 00:12:39.600
FELIPE HOFFA: That's one way.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.100
Now, Cloud Pub/Sub
doesn't output data

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:45.670
straight int BigQuery.

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY:OK.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:50.040
FELIPE HOFFA: Don't ask
me why, but maybe one day.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:51.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Lovely.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:52.390
We'll fix that.

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:55.450
FELIPE HOFFA: One
of our customers

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.120
wrote a very nice
blog post where

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.600
they used Pub/Sub to ingest
a lot of streaming data.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:03.260
They found a lot
goes into BigQuery.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.176
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:04.176 --> 00:13:06.700
FELIPE HOFFA: Now, in the
middle they used Dataflow.

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:07.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Oh, interesting.

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:09.090
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes,
Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:10.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Actually, since we're

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:12.810
already discussing
about Cloud Dataflow,

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:14.320
so BigQuery is one
of the products

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:19.550
that you can do big data
processing with Google Cloud

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:20.400
Platform.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.230
What about Cloud Dataflow?

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:25.300
What is the difference and why I
would you use one or the other?

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:26.050
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes.

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:28.430
So if you have data,
you have questions

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:32.567
that you can express with
SQL, BigQuery is the place.

00:13:32.567 --> 00:13:33.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:34.115
Cool.

00:13:34.115 --> 00:13:35.600
FELIPE HOFFA: Now,
with Dataflow,

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.230
we are able to solve
different kinds of problems

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:45.010
like where you would code-- you
would use java code to write

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:48.910
more complicated stages.

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:51.030
MARK MANDEL: Is it
more like MapReduce or?

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:51.821
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:13:51.821 --> 00:13:55.020
Dataflow is the new MapReduce.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:57.984
We published the MapReduce
paper like 10 years ago?

00:13:57.984 --> 00:13:58.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:00.270
Maybe more.

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:01.040
It was awhile ago.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:02.710
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:04.520
FELIPE HOFFA: And since
then, we published

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:06.357
two very interesting papers.

00:14:06.357 --> 00:14:10.266
One is Flume-- FlumeJava.

00:14:10.266 --> 00:14:12.870
And that talks about
how we orchestrate

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:18.036
these pipelines of MapReducers
and optimize them on the way.

00:14:18.036 --> 00:14:23.142
And also the MillWheel paper
that deals with streaming data.

00:14:23.142 --> 00:14:30.027
And Dataflow is our service that
encapsulates these two ideas.

00:14:30.027 --> 00:14:30.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:34.660
FELIPE HOFFA: How do we write
a smart pipeline of MapReduce

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:36.030
processes?

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:38.495
So MapReduce has its problems.

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:45.030
If you really want your
MapReduce processes to go fast,

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:47.150
you have to do a lot
of manual optimization.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:53.560
And Dataflow takes care of that.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.390
And you just write
your pipelines

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:58.592
as you could write
them mentally.

00:14:58.592 --> 00:15:01.580
And then the optimization
is part of the process.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:04.262
You don't have to care about
optimizing things manually.

00:15:04.262 --> 00:15:05.720
MARK MANDEL: So
what sort of things

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:08.090
would I be using Dataflow for?

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:10.150
Like if I can get my
data to be [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:10.150 --> 00:15:12.960
and process it there, like can
you give me an example of like,

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:14.390
is it like real time?

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:16.999
Or is it like what sort of
stuff am I doing with Dataflow?

00:15:16.999 --> 00:15:17.790
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:21.080
So Dataflow has two--
we'll say two modes.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.808
One is a batch mode--

00:15:22.808 --> 00:15:23.474
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:15:23.474 --> 00:15:23.670
And what is that?

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:25.250
FELIPE HOFFA: And one
is a streaming mode.

00:15:25.250 --> 00:15:26.833
MARK MANDEL: So
what's the difference?

00:15:28.778 --> 00:15:31.354
FELIPE HOFFA: Well, just
to-- the difference-

00:15:31.354 --> 00:15:32.270
what's the difference?

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:41.980
Part of the charm of Dataflow
is that your same code could

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:43.374
work for both types of data.

00:15:43.374 --> 00:15:44.040
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:45.415
FELIPE HOFFA:
Usually people have

00:15:45.415 --> 00:15:48.810
to deal with one system
for batch processing

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.620
and one system for
streaming processes.

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:55.400
With Dataflow, you can do both.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:58.300
Just to give you a simple
example of what I use Dataflow

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:04.640
for, when I found some web
logs that were in binary format

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:07.470
that I wanted to
load into BigQuery,

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:09.370
I needed to write some code.

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:11.750
The logs were massive,
it was millions of rows.

00:16:11.750 --> 00:16:13.770
So I had to process
them in parallel.

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:18.000
So I could write just a
simple script in Java,

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:18.970
inside Dataflow.

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:24.672
And Dataflow took care of doing
this process of splitting it

00:16:24.672 --> 00:16:27.954
and writing it into
many different servers.

00:16:27.954 --> 00:16:28.620
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:30.390
So using it as sort of--
do data transformations.

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:31.180
FELIPE HOFFA: Data--

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:32.990
MARK MANDEL: Just massive
data transformations.

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:33.930
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:36.110
Dataflow takes care of
the parrellization--

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:36.860
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:39.350
FELIPE HOFFA: And making
this process smart..

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:42.640
But what's really brilliant
about Dataflow too,

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:44.990
is how it treats streaming data.

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:46.140
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:48.890
FELIPE HOFFA: So I mentioned
that you can stream data

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:52.275
into BigQuery and you can
analyze it later, immediately,

00:16:52.275 --> 00:16:53.790
with queries.

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:57.910
But sometimes you want run
some online algorithms.

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:00.669
I love the Wikipedia
example where

00:17:00.669 --> 00:17:01.960
you can look at the change log.

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:04.420
So how many-- what
pages are people

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:07.004
changing at any given time?

00:17:07.004 --> 00:17:11.220
And the question there
is, from a stream of--

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:15.319
like you have a list of
what pages are changing

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:16.760
on Wikipedia at any time.

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:20.625
How can you find what
are relevant use?

00:17:20.625 --> 00:17:22.391
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:17:22.391 --> 00:17:24.950
FELIPE HOFFA: So how can you
tell this change is important,

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:26.765
versus this change is not.

00:17:26.765 --> 00:17:27.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.440
So you could do something quite
similar like trending topics

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:32.477
on Twitter and stuff like that.

00:17:32.477 --> 00:17:33.560
That's really interesting.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:34.160
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:34.750
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:36.291
So there's a Googler,
Thomas Steiner,

00:17:36.291 --> 00:17:37.930
that wrote a paper on this.

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:40.590
And he looks at three things.

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:43.220
Is this page-- how
many changes this page

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:44.437
have had in the last minute?

00:17:44.437 --> 00:17:45.145
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:17:45.145 --> 00:17:49.160
FELIPE HOFFA: And how
many different people

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:52.160
are changing it.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:58.000
And there was a third
factor that I-- so it's

00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:59.965
how many changes,
how many people,

00:17:59.965 --> 00:18:02.540
and how frequent
are these changes.

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:05.430
Once we for surpass
those thresholds,

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:08.070
we can say, oh, now we
have something interesting.

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:10.870
Like we have 50 people at the
same time doing that, changes.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:12.735
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, yep.

00:18:12.735 --> 00:18:14.110
FELIPE HOFFA: I
would love to run

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:16.570
that I algorithm on
a stream of data,

00:18:16.570 --> 00:18:18.644
just to get real time results.

00:18:18.644 --> 00:18:19.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:20.940
That'd be really interesting.

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:25.000
I can even imagine using
that for monitoring

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:26.180
of your own application.

00:18:26.180 --> 00:18:28.700
Like trying to understand--
like same thing

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:32.060
you can do monitoring on Google
Cloud Platform of our logs.

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:34.430
And see like, oh, maybe
the traffic is too high.

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:35.519
Send me an alarm.

00:18:35.519 --> 00:18:37.185
Or you could do
something more advanced.

00:18:37.185 --> 00:18:38.335
MARK MANDEL: Like
business metrics.

00:18:38.335 --> 00:18:38.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:39.620
Business metrics.

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:42.161
MARK MANDEL: How much money is
being sold-- like being spent,

00:18:42.161 --> 00:18:43.474
and their products being sold.

00:18:43.474 --> 00:18:44.390
So that sort of stuff.

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:45.950
FELIPE HOFFA: So
with Dataflow, you

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:49.890
have the ability to define
some rules over some window

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:52.770
sessions, time.

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:55.250
It makes it very, very
easy to start defining

00:18:55.250 --> 00:18:56.209
that kind of processes.

00:18:56.209 --> 00:18:58.333
MARK MANDEL: Now I want to
play with Dataflow more.

00:18:58.333 --> 00:18:59.425
FELIPE HOFFA: Oh, yes.

00:18:59.425 --> 00:19:02.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Dataflow
is actually the only reason

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:05.340
why I've written Java lately.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:07.880
Yeah, which you know
it's going-- yeah.

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:09.570
That's a big statement for me.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:12.194
FELIPE HOFFA: We also
announced a Python Dataflow.

00:19:12.194 --> 00:19:13.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:14.040
I heard about that.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:15.817
FELIPE HOFFA: But
still not there.

00:19:15.817 --> 00:19:16.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:18.730
FELIPE HOFFA: So write
java in the meantime.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:19.563
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:19:19.563 --> 00:19:24.120
So we've discussed two things,
BigQuery and Cloud Dataflow.

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:26.680
I know-- and now that
you mentioned Python,

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:31.327
you made me think about
it-- it's the Cloud Datalab.

00:19:31.327 --> 00:19:32.660
FELIPE HOFFA: Datalab-- Datalab.

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:33.220
Yes.

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:34.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
depending on who you are.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.315
MARK MANDEL: Depending on
which accent in the room

00:19:35.315 --> 00:19:36.390
you're going to go with.

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:36.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:39.310
By the other way, accent-wise
today, we're doing--

00:19:39.310 --> 00:19:40.780
MARK MANDEL: We're
almost at a-- we

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:42.650
