00:32:47.374 --> 00:32:48.915
the things that he's
been working on.

00:32:48.915 --> 00:32:49.831
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:32:49.831 --> 00:32:50.707
That was lots of fun.

00:32:50.707 --> 00:32:51.747
MARK MANDEL: Lots of fun.

00:32:51.747 --> 00:32:54.090
But we have a really interesting
question of the week,

00:32:54.090 --> 00:32:55.990
yet again.

00:32:55.990 --> 00:32:59.960
Started with a conversation
I had with Andrew Watson.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:03.095
He was talking about sort of
environment variables in App

00:33:03.095 --> 00:33:04.020
Engine.

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:05.450
And it sort of
expanded from there

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:09.280
into sort of-- I've got like
a password, or a credential,

00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:10.340
or a secret key.

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:13.794
You know, I need to store that
somewhere safe with the Google

00:33:13.794 --> 00:33:15.460
Cloud Platform and
be able to share that

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:16.930
with a variety of services.

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:18.622
What sort of the
ways I can do that?

00:33:18.622 --> 00:33:20.830
Or maybe even just what's
the best way of doing that?

00:33:20.830 --> 00:33:23.079
Now I know you've got some
opinions on this, Francesc.

00:33:23.079 --> 00:33:25.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I do
have some opinions.

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:29.120
Yeah, so it really depends on
what you are actually doing.

00:33:29.120 --> 00:33:31.570
So I know that, for instance,
if you're in App Engine--

00:33:31.570 --> 00:33:31.770
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:31.770 --> 00:33:34.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There is App
Engine environment variables--

00:33:34.040 --> 00:33:34.530
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:35.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
--that works very well.

00:33:35.920 --> 00:33:37.920
But it will be available
only for App Engine.

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:38.765
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:33:38.765 --> 00:33:40.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If
you're in Kubernetes--

00:33:40.370 --> 00:33:40.850
MARK MANDEL: In Secrets.

00:33:40.850 --> 00:33:41.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:33:41.430 --> 00:33:42.555
There's Kubernetes secrets.

00:33:42.555 --> 00:33:43.305
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:43.305 --> 00:33:45.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that's
a very good solution,

00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:48.510
when you're running
everything in Kubernetes.

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:52.930
But by default, my
go-to way of storing

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:55.830
stuff-- secrets, but
not only secrets.

00:33:55.830 --> 00:33:59.120
Also configuration and
all of those things

00:33:59.120 --> 00:34:01.660
that you want to
share across all

00:34:01.660 --> 00:34:05.592
of the agents of your system,
is the metadata service.

00:34:05.592 --> 00:34:07.300
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that
sounds interesting.

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:08.414
Tell me more about what that is.

00:34:08.414 --> 00:34:10.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So it's
something really simple.

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:13.710
It's basically a way
to store metadata.

00:34:13.710 --> 00:34:15.190
MARK MANDEL: Like
key value pairs.

00:34:15.190 --> 00:34:16.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Key
value pairs, exactly.

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:19.219
So you have a key, which is a
string; and then a value, which

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:19.730
is a string.

00:34:19.730 --> 00:34:20.230
That's it.

00:34:20.230 --> 00:34:20.880
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:22.713
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
the good thing about it

00:34:22.713 --> 00:34:25.234
is that it's actually very safe.

00:34:25.234 --> 00:34:25.900
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:27.608
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
very simple to use.

00:34:27.608 --> 00:34:29.739
It has a REST API.

00:34:29.739 --> 00:34:31.591
And it gives authentication.

00:34:31.591 --> 00:34:32.340
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:36.002
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you're
going to need some scopes

00:34:36.002 --> 00:34:37.210
to access it, and everything.

00:34:37.210 --> 00:34:41.239
But if you're doing it from
inside of a Compute Engine,

00:34:41.239 --> 00:34:43.190
by default you're already
authorized to do it.

00:34:43.190 --> 00:34:43.690
MARK MANDEL: That's nice.

00:34:43.690 --> 00:34:44.489
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
You're actually going

00:34:44.489 --> 00:34:46.111
to be hitting an
internal endpoint.

00:34:46.111 --> 00:34:46.860
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:34:46.860 --> 00:34:50.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So even just
[? Curl ?] will work for you.

00:34:50.500 --> 00:34:53.500
If you're doing it from
outside of Compute Engine,

00:34:53.500 --> 00:34:56.429
you will need to use
the REST API, that

00:34:56.429 --> 00:35:00.230
then allows you to
access the same API

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:01.367
but with authentication.

00:35:01.367 --> 00:35:02.700
MARK MANDEL: That's really cool.

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:05.810
So I only thought you
could access the metadata

00:35:05.810 --> 00:35:08.379
stuff basically from within
Google Compute Engine.

00:35:08.379 --> 00:35:09.920
But you saying,
like, we can actually

00:35:09.920 --> 00:35:10.845
access that from anywhere?

00:35:10.845 --> 00:35:12.178
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I did that too.

00:35:12.178 --> 00:35:14.060
I did think so, too.

00:35:14.060 --> 00:35:17.000
And actually, while
preparing this podcast,

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:20.170
one of our colleagues,
Sandeep, showed us

00:35:20.170 --> 00:35:21.712
that we were wrong,
which is awesome.

00:35:21.712 --> 00:35:22.794
MARK MANDEL: That's great.

00:35:22.794 --> 00:35:23.586
I love being wrong.

00:35:23.586 --> 00:35:25.085
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It actually makes--

00:35:25.085 --> 00:35:26.430
it makes your life much easier.

00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:30.480
Basically, you just keep all
your data in the Compute Engine

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:33.050
metadata service and
you can access it

00:35:33.050 --> 00:35:34.682
from wherever you want.

00:35:34.682 --> 00:35:36.390
MARK MANDEL: That's
really, really handy.

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:37.848
Just add whatever
you want in there

00:35:37.848 --> 00:35:40.180
and you just need
to know it's secure.

00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:42.410
It's encrypted both
ways and on storage too.

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:43.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
incredibly simple.

00:35:43.340 --> 00:35:43.550
Yeah.

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:44.175
It's encrypted.

00:35:44.175 --> 00:35:46.340
And not only a REST.

00:35:46.340 --> 00:35:49.490
So once we store it, we store it
encrypted, but also in transit.

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:54.920
So while it's going from the
service to your instance,

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:57.010
it will also be encrypted there.

00:35:57.010 --> 00:36:00.180
So it's really,
really simple to use.

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:04.260
If you had other-- other things
that you wanted to share,

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:07.130
like there were like--
imagine it's not just

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:08.150
something quite small.

00:36:08.150 --> 00:36:10.590
It's, I don't know, a
big file or something

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:13.160
that you want to keep
secret, then probably

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:14.145
Google Cloud Storage.

00:36:14.145 --> 00:36:16.120
That just depends--
way to do it,

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:18.880
because then you don't have
limitations on the size.

00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:20.430
You can keep whatever you want.

00:36:20.430 --> 00:36:23.140
And it's also very
secure, it's encrypted.

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:25.360
It's going to require
you authentication,

00:36:25.360 --> 00:36:27.320
and all of the same
things, just bigger.

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:28.130
MARK MANDEL: By
the same token, I

00:36:28.130 --> 00:36:30.200
suppose you could also use
Datastore or even Cloud SQL

00:36:30.200 --> 00:36:30.810
if you really wanted to.

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:31.596
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:36:31.596 --> 00:36:33.390
MARK MANDEL: But it sounds
like the metadata API,

00:36:33.390 --> 00:36:35.650
I mean that sounds like it's
kind of built for this stuff.

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:37.000
That sounds like a
really good solution.

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:38.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's the
simplest way to do it.

00:36:38.090 --> 00:36:40.950
And yeah, I guess one of
the cool things of this

00:36:40.950 --> 00:36:44.110
is that, yeah, it also works
outside of Compute Engine,

00:36:44.110 --> 00:36:46.000
so you can access it
through a REST API.

00:36:46.000 --> 00:36:47.315
MARK MANDEL: That
is very, very cool.

00:36:47.315 --> 00:36:47.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:48.844
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

